These are notes from the Fraud Detection in the Banking Sector lecture hosted by Wellington Muvunduke on the 4th of May:
  What financial crime looks like in the real world
    Illegal acts involving money, systems or assets to produce illicit gain.
  Traditional cybersecurity
    Protect systems from intrusion
    Attacker uses exploits and malware
    Perimeter based controls are primary
    Alert = anomalous system behaviour
    Response time: hours to days
  Financial fraud
    Abuse of legitimate access
    Attacker uses stolen credentials or deception
    Social engineering is the primary vector
    Alert = anomalous human behaviour
    Response time: seconds to minutes
  Account takeover
    Attacker gains access to a customer account via phishing, credential stuffing, or SIM swap. Victim is locked out of their own account.
    1. Credential harvesting: phishing for email or fake login page captures username and password. Or bought from a data breach marketplace.
    2. Account access: attacker logs in from a new device or unknown IP. System may flag it, but not always block it.
    3. Recon: Attacker reviews balances, linked payees, contact details.
    4. Social engineering: may call the bank impersonating the customer, or call the customer impersonating the bank, to bypass MFA.
    5. Fund transfer: rapid transfers to mule accounts. Often multiple smaller transactions to avoid triggering velocity thresholds.
  Authorised push payment
    Customer is deceived into authorising a payment to a fraudster- impersonating banks, government, or even family members.
  Identity theft
    Fraudster opens accounts or applies for credit using stolen personal info. Victim may not know for weeks of months.
  Card fraud
    unauthorised use of card details. Card not present fraud is dominant in online transactions.
  Insider threats
    Employees with system access misuse their position to commit fraud or assist external actors- one of the hardest to detect.
  Money laundering
    Making illicit funds appear legitimate. Banks are legally required to detect and report suspicious activity.
  
  Application fraud
    Occurs when someone uses false, stolen or synthetic information to apply for financial products, such as loans, credit cards or bank accounts.
  Romance and pig butchering
    Long term deception where fraudster builds trust over weeks before requesting funds.
  Investment scams
    Fake trading platforms, crypto schemes and too good to be true returns.
  Business email comprimise
    Impersonating a CFO or supplier via email to redirect payments.
  SIM swap attacks
    Fraudster convinces a telco to transfer a victim's phone number to a new SIM, bypassing SMS based MFA.
  Synthetic identity fraud
    Fraudster creates a fake identity by blending real and fabricated data. Builds credit over time then vanishes.

  Tools and techniques used to detect suspicious behaviour
    Technical signals
      Device fingerprinting
      Geolocation anomalies
      Velocity checks- rapid repeated actions
      Behavioural biometrics
      New device or unrecognised login
    Behavioural signals
      Change in transaction patterns
      New payee creation
      Unusual transaction timing
      Large or out-of-character payment amounts
      Deviation from customer's historical baseline

  Rule based detection
    If X then alert. Fast and explainable, but rigid. Often learned to work around.
  Mahcine learning models
    Trained on historical fraud data to score transactions for risk. Catches complex patterns no rule could encode. Powerful but black boxes.
    Lots of false positives.
  Risk scoring engines
    Combines multiple signals into a single risk score per transaction or session. High score = flagged for analyst review
    Balances automation with human judgement.
  Hybrid approaches
    Most modern fraud systems combine rules with ML and analyst review. No single method catches everything. The analyst remains the final decision maker.

  Alert generated -> Case created -> Signal review -> Customer contact -> Decision
  False positive = legitimate action flagged as fraud.
  False negative = fraud goes undetected -> customer loses money + bank may be liable

  How AI is being weaponised
    AI generated phishing: perfectly written, hyper-personalised phishing emails at massive scale. No more spelling mistakes or obvious tells. Scrapes LinkedIn and 
    social media.
    Voice cloning: real-time voice synthesis can now impersonate family members, bank staff or government officials. OFTEN CAN DETECT BECAUSE COMPUTERS DON'T BREATHE.
    Deepfake indetity verification: fake live videos to bypass Know Your Customer checks at accound opening.
    Automated scam scaling: AI bots can run thousands of scam attempts simultaneously, messaging platforms, dating apps, investment forums. The cost per attempt has 
    dropped to near zero.
  AI defense
    Anomaly detection at scale: ML models itendify behaviour across millions of transactions in real time.
    Behavioural profiling: AI builds a unique baseline for each custoemr based on spending patterns, device usage and timing. Any deviation automatically raises a flag.
    Real time risk scoring: every transaction gets a risk score milliseconds before it is approved. High risk transactions are paused for review or declined.
    Analyst decision support: AI surfaces the most relevant signals to the analyst so they can make faster, more accurate decisions.
